#module "logs"
'' std/logs: logging -- the overview of the logging module
''
''     #import "std/logs"
''     using logs
''
''     dim con as ConsoleSink
''     dim lg  as Logger
''     lg.AddSink( @con )
''     lg.Info( "server started" )
''     lg.Warn( ).AddText( "user", u ).AddInt( "attempts", n ).Msg( "login failed" )
''
'' Specified in C:\dev\docs\logging\. Mirrors std/containers' containers.tiko and
'' std/fs' fs.tiko: one #import gets the whole module.
''
'' LIBRARY ONLY. No compiler change and no logging C in the runtime -- unlike
'' std/fs, every line of this module is FreeBASIC.
''
'' | File            | RFC     | What it is                                  |
'' |-----------------|---------|---------------------------------------------|
'' | logrecord.tiko  | 0001    | LogLevel, LogValue, LogField, LogRecord     |
'' | logrender.tiko  | 0003    | Renderer, TextRenderer, JsonRenderer        |
'' | logerror.tiko   | 0005    | OnLogError, the OS plumbing                 |
'' | logsink.tiko    | 0004    | Sink, ConsoleSink                           |
'' | logfile.tiko    | 0004    | FileSink, RotatePolicy                      |
'' | logasync.tiko   | 0005    | AsyncSink, OverflowPolicy                   |
'' | logger.tiko     | 0002    | Logger, LogEvent                            |
'' | logdefault.tiko | 0002    | DefaultLogger( ), LogInit, LogShutdown      |
'' | logmacros.tiko  | 0002    | LogInfo( m ), LogEventWarn( f, m ), ...     |
''
'' PHASES 1-6.
''
'' THE MACROS ARE PART OF THE MODULE: logmacros.tiko's logs.LogInfo( m ) and the rest, with
'' call-site capture and compile-time elision, exported and used qualified. (As fb/logmacros.bi
'' they were global FB_LOG_* macros in an opt-in header: macros cannot be namespaced.)
''
'' THE DEFAULT LOGGER NEEDS NOTHING FROM YOU: std/logs holds it (logdefault.tiko), and the
'' FB_LOG_IMPLEMENT( ) a header needed is gone. Explicit Logger instances need nothing either.
''
'' Nothing here is a keyword. `Logger`, `Sink` and the rest stay available as
'' your own identifiers until you say `using logs` -- and the macros stay
'' qualified even then.


